Note that the is twice as sensitive as  other  volume  variables.
The  hardware  volume  changes  the  value.  If an external input
source is unmuted by setting corresponding variable to the device
is never put in global power down or power save  mode.   This  is
because if the device is in global power down or power save mode,
the  output  is  automatically  muted.   All  the  external input
sources (CD playback, line input, speaker, and MIC) are muted  by
default.  The variables does not exists on OPL3‐SA2.  The and are
enhancement  only, and any values below the center position (128)
don’t take any effect.  The driver is capable of power management
on the OPL3‐SAx devices.  The following modes can be selected  by
setting  variable  of to and respectively.  When a subpart of the
device is unused, the part is power‐down after a  timeout  period
(specified  by variable of in seconds).  When all the subparts of
the device are unused, and all the  external  input  sources  are
muted,  the  driver puts the device in mode.  On the global power
down mode, the power consumption  is  minimized  (10μA  (SA3)  or
200μA  (SA2)  typ.), but the click noise on power up/down the de‐
vice is rather loud.  This mode should not  be  used  with  head‐
phones or hi-fi audio systems, or your ears or the systems may be
damaged.   When  a  subpart  of the device is unused, the part is
powered‐down after a timeout period (specified by variable of  in
seconds).   When  all  the subparts of the device are unused, and
all the external input sources are muted, the driver put the  de‐
vice  in  mode.  In power save mode, the power consumption is re‐
duced (5mA typ.).  The click noise on power up/down of the device
is very small, but this operation  requires  muting/unmuting  the
device,  which  make  some  noise.  In order to reduce the noise,
setting the master volume at the small value is effective.   Once
the  device is powered‐up, it remains on after the use of the de‐
vice.  Once a subpart of the device is powered‐up, it  shall  not
be power‐down.  This mode minimizes click noises on power switch‐
ing,  but maximizes power consumption (30‐100mA).  On suspending,
the device is put into power‐save state.  The device  driver  ap‐
peared in Although the parameters of the device are saved and re‐
stored  on  suspend/resume,  the DMA state is not restored.  That
is, if the system suspends during playback, this is not continued
after suspend/resume cycle.  The joystick port is not under power
management.  If a device is configured, the device will never  be
put  in  global  power down or power save mode.  The external de‐
vices, such as Zoomed Video port, OPL4‐ML/2,  modem,  and  CD‐ROM
are not supported.
